Given surface: \[z^2 = 2x^2 - y^2\] Define \[F(x,y,z)=2x^2-y^2-z^2\] Then the normal vector to the surface is given by \[\nabla F = \left( \frac{\partial F}{\partial x} \right)\hat{i}
+ \left( \frac{\partial F}{\partial y} \right)\hat{j}
+ \left( \frac{\partial F}{\partial z} \right)\hat{k}\] \[\nabla F = 4x\hat{i}-2y\hat{j}-2z\hat{k}\] The point is \[\vec{P}=\hat{i}+\sqrt{2}\hat{k}\] Hence, \[x=1,\qquad y=0,\qquad z=\sqrt{2}\] Substituting: \[\nabla F = 4\hat{i}-2\sqrt{2}\hat{k}\] Magnitude: \[|\nabla F|
=
\sqrt{4^2+(2\sqrt{2})^2}
=
\sqrt{16+8}
=
2\sqrt{6}\] Therefore unit normal vector is \[\hat{n}
=
\frac{4\hat{i}-2\sqrt{2}\hat{k}}{2\sqrt{6}}
=
\frac{2\hat{i}-\sqrt{2}\hat{k}}{\sqrt{6}}\] Rationalizing: \[\hat{n}
=
\frac{\sqrt{2}\hat{i}-\hat{k}}{\sqrt{3}}\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) $\frac{\sqrt{2}\hat{i}-\hat{k}}{\sqrt{3}}$}}\]

For the real baseband signal \[x(t)=e^{-2t}, \qquad t\ge 0\] its Fourier transform is \[X(f)=\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-2t}e^{-j2\pi ft}\,dt
=\frac{1}{2+j2\pi f}.\] Hence, \[|X(f)|^2=\frac{1}{4+4\pi^2f^2}
=\frac{1}{4(1+\pi^2f^2)}.\] Using Parseval's theorem, the fraction of energy contained in the frequency range \(-B\le f\le B\) is \[\frac{\displaystyle\int_{-B}^{B}|X(f)|^2\,df}
{\displaystyle\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|X(f)|^2\,df}
=0.99.\] Now, \[\int\frac{df}{1+\pi^2f^2}
=\frac{1}{\pi}\tan^{-1}(\pi f).\] Therefore, \[\frac{2\cdot\frac{1}{4\pi}\tan^{-1}(\pi B)}
{\frac{1}{4}}
=\frac{2}{\pi}\tan^{-1}(\pi B)=0.99.\] Hence, \[\tan^{-1}(\pi B)=0.495\pi.\] Using \[\tan\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2}-\theta\right)=\cot\theta,\] with \(\theta=0.005\pi,\) \[\pi B=\tan(0.495\pi)
=\cot(0.005\pi)
\approx 63.66.\] Thus, \[B\approx\frac{63.66}{\pi}\,\text{Hz}
\approx20.26\,\text{Hz}.\] Since \[\frac{63}{\pi}&lt;\frac{63.66}{\pi}&lt;\frac{64}{\pi},\] the correct option is obtained. \[\boxed{\text{(b) }\dfrac{63}{\pi}\,\text{Hz}&lt;B&lt;\dfrac{64}{\pi}\,\text{Hz}}\]

A wavelength is absorbed by a semiconductor if the photon energy exceeds the bandgap: \[\lambda \leq \frac{1.24}{E_G}\,\mu\text{m}\] Substituting \(E_G = 1.3\,\text{eV}\): \[\lambda \leq \frac{1.24}{1.3}\,\mu\text{m} = 0.953\,\mu\text{m} = 953\,\text{nm}\] Since the device is an LED, its emission spectrum is centered around the bandgap wavelength (953 nm) with an approximate bandwidth of \(\pm 20\,\text{nm}\). Therefore, the LED emits light roughly in the range 933 nm to 973 nm . Among the given options, only 950 ± 20 nm lies within the emission spectrum. Hence, the LED will <strong>not emit</strong>wavelengths corresponding to 1410 \(\pm 20\,\text{nm}\), 1090 \(\pm 20\,\text{nm}\), and 510 \(\pm 20\,\text{nm}\). \[\boxed{(A)\,1410 \pm 20 \text{ nm}}
        \boxed{(B)\,1090 \pm 20 \text{ nm}}
        \boxed{(D)\,510 \pm 20 \text{ nm}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong> : A wavelength is absorbed by a semiconductor if the photon energy exceeds the bandgap: \(\lambda \leq \dfrac{1.24}{E_G}\,\mu\text{m}\) . Thus, photons with wavelengths much shorter than the bandgap wavelength (for example, 510 nm in this question) can still be absorbed.</li><li><strong>Emission</strong> : An LED emits photons due to electron-hole recombination, and the emitted photon energy is approximately equal to the bandgap energy. Therefore, the emission spectrum is centered around the bandgap wavelength with a finite spectral width.</li></ul></div>

Given input signal \[v_i(t) = 6 \sin(\omega t)\] So maximum input = \(+6\,\text{V}\) and two diodes act as clippers. <br/><strong>Case 1:</strong> During negative half cycle D1 is <strong>OFF</strong>, D2 is <strong>ON</strong> <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> Hence \(V_o(t) = -4\, \text{V}\). <br/><strong>Case 2:</strong> During positive half cycle D1 is <strong>ON</strong>, D2 is <strong>OFF</strong> <img src="img/2b5c699d6d54363f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,384px)"> \[I = \frac{6 - 2}{20\,\text{k}\Omega} = \frac{4}{20\,\text{k}\Omega} = 0.2\,\text{mA}\] \[V_{0 \max} = I \times 10\,\text{K} + 2\,\text{V}\] \[= 0.2\,\text{mA} \times 10\,\text{K} + 2\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{
 V_{0 \max} = 4\,\text{V}
}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The above circuit is a 2-way clipper where the output voltage is clipped on both polarities due to diodes.</li><li>Determine the voltages at both terminals of diode to find out whether it is ON or OFF.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Since \(V_{i \max}\) is \(6 V\) don't assume \(V_{0 \max}\) is also \(6 V\) as it is clipped.</li></ul></div>

Based on the provided circuit diagram of the multiplexer, we can derive the Boolean expression using two different approaches. <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>We can see \(I_0\) and \(I_2\) are directly connected to high; thus, if those are selected we will directly get output. Thus, we will get: \[= \overline{x}\overline{z} + x\overline{z}\] If \(I_3\) is selected then we will get output high for \(y\) high, thus minterm added: \[= xyz\] Final expression: \[\begin{aligned}
&amp; = \overline{x}\overline{z} + x\overline{z} + xyz &amp;&amp; \\
&amp; = \overline{z}(\overline{x} + x) + xyz \quad \dots (\because \overline{x} + x = 1) &amp;&amp; \\
&amp; = \overline{z} + xyz &amp;&amp; \\
&amp; = \overline{z} + xy \quad \dots (\text{By redundant term theorem}) &amp;&amp;
\end{aligned}\]</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div>By analyzing the diagram we can generate the following truth table: <div class="table-scroll"><table class="qtable"><tr><td>\(x\)</td><td>\(z\)</td><td>\(y\)</td><td>\(f(x, y, z)\)</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr></table></div> K-map for the same will be: <img src="img/b16bb4613c011ec7.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)"> Thus, \(\overline{z} + xy\).</div> \[\boxed{(C)xy + \overline{z}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Ensure that the select lines \(x\) and \(z\) are correctly mapped to the multiplexer inputs \(S_1\) and \(S_0\).</li></ul></div>

Given state space model is, \[\dot{x}=Ax+Bu\] where, \[A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-4 &amp; -1.5\\
4 &amp; 0
\end{array}\right],
\qquad
B=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
2\\
0
\end{array}\right]\] and output equation is, \[y=Cx\] where, \[C=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1.5 &amp; 0.625
\end{array}\right]\] Since there is no direct transmission term, we have \[D=0\] Transfer function of the system is given by \[G(s)=\frac{Y(s)}{U(s)}
=
C(sI-A)^{-1}B+D\] First, compute \(sI-A\), \[sI-A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s+4 &amp; 1.5\\
-4 &amp; s
\end{array}\right]\] Determinant is, \[|sI-A|
=
\left|\begin{array}{cc}
s+4 &amp; 1.5\\
-4 &amp; s
\end{array}\right|\] \[=(s+4)(s)-(-4)(1.5)\] \[=s^2+4s+6\] Hence, \[(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{s^2+4s+6}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s &amp; -1.5\\
4 &amp; s+4
\end{array}\right]\] Now multiply \((sI-A)^{-1}\) with \(B\), \[(sI-A)^{-1}B
=
\frac{1}{s^2+4s+6}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s &amp; -1.5\\
4 &amp; s+4
\end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c}
2\\
0
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\frac{1}{s^2+4s+6}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
2s\\
8
\end{array}\right]\] Multiplying with matrix \(C\), \[G(s)
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1.5 &amp; 0.625
\end{array}\right]
\frac{1}{s^2+4s+6}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
2s\\
8
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\frac{1}{s^2+4s+6}
\left[
(1.5)(2s)+(0.625)(8)
\right]
=
\frac{1}{s^2+4s+6}
\left[
3s+5
\right]
=
\frac{3s+5}{s^2+4s+6}\] \[\boxed{\text{(C) }
\frac{3s+5}{s^2+4s+6}
}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Transfer function from state space model is obtained using \[G(s)=C(sI-A)^{-1}B+D\]</li></ul></div>

We need to convert the direct speech sentence ``Suresh said, `I did it yesterday','' into indirect (reported) speech. To convert this sentence accurately, we apply four structural grammar rule modifications: <ol><li><strong>Reporting verb tense:</strong> The past tense ``said'' is maintained and introduces the subordinate clause with the conjunction ``said that''.</li><li><strong>Pronoun shift:</strong> The first-person pronoun ``I'' shifts to the third-person singular pronoun ``he'' to align with the subject (Suresh).</li><li><strong>Tense backshift:</strong> The simple past tense verb ``did'' shifts one step further back into the past perfect tense as ``had done''.</li><li><strong>Time adverb shift:</strong> The relative temporal adverb ``yesterday'' transforms into the absolute reporting baseline ``the day before''.</li></ol> Combining these updates maps to the structural template: \[\underbrace{\text{Suresh said}}_{\text{reporting verb}}
  \underbrace{\text{ that }}_{\text{conjunction}}
  \underbrace{\text{he}}_{\text{I}\to\text{he}}
  \underbrace{\text{ had done }}_{\text{did}\to\text{had done}}
  \text{it }
  \underbrace{\text{the day before}}_{\text{yesterday}\to\text{the day before}}
  \text{.}\] This complete syntactic shift matches Option D. \[\boxed{\text{Option D. Suresh said that he had done it the day before.}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Past simple forms must shift to past perfect structures in standard indirect reporting.</li><li>Relative time frames like ``yesterday'' shift to ``the day before'' or ``the previous day''.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li><strong>Option A</strong> fails because it retains the un-shifted pronoun ``I''.</li><li><strong>Options B and C</strong> are incorrect because they change the past reporting verb into the present-tense ``says''.</li></ul></div>
